  "chunks": [
    {
      "chunk_type": "isnad",
      "arabic_text": "مُحَمَّدُ بْنُ يَحْيَى عَنْ أَحْمَدَ بْنِ مُحَمَّدٍ عَنِ الْحَسَنِ بْنِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ فَضَّالٍ عَنْ عَبْدِ اللَّهِ بْنِ بُكَيْرٍ عَنْ عُبَيْدِ بْنِ زُرَارَةَ قَالَ",
      "translations": {
        "en": "Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Ahmad ibn Muhammad, from Al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Fazzal, from Abdullah ibn Bukayr, from Ubayd ibn Zurara, who said:"
      }
    },
    {
      "chunk_type": "body",
      "arabic_text": "سَأَلْتُ أَبَا عَبْدِ اللَّهِ ( عليه السلام ) عَنِ الرَّجُلِ يُزَوِّجُ ابْنَهُ وَ هُوَ صَغِيرٌ قَالَ إِنْ كَانَ لِابْنِهِ مَالٌ فَعَلَيْهِ الْمَهْرُ وَ إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لِلِابْنِ مَالٌ فَالْأَبُ ضَامِنُ الْمَهْرِ ضَمِنَ أَوْ لَمْ يَضْمَنْ",
      "translations": {
        "en": "I asked Abu Abdullah (peace be upon him) about the man who marries off his son while he is young. He (peace be upon him) said: If the son has wealth, then the dower is upon him. And if the son does not have wealth, then the father is responsible for the dower, whether he undertakes the responsibility or does not undertake it."
      }
    }
  ],
  "translations": {
    "en": {
      "summary": "This hadith establishes the legal ruling regarding the dower (mahr) for a young son who is married by his father. It dictates that if the son possesses wealth, the dower is his responsibility; otherwise, the father bears the liability for the dower regardless of whether he explicitly assumed the obligation.",
      "seo_question": "Who is responsible for paying the dower (mahr) if a father marries off his minor son?",
      "key_terms": {
        "يُزَوِّجُ": "marries off (a son)",
        "صَغِيرٌ": "young/minor",
        "الغير بالغ (minor/not yet mature in financial independence context here, though physically young, the context implies lack of independent wealth/status for marriage contract liability in this specific fiqh ruling context, often interpreted as minor or lacking independent means in classical fiqh discussions of paternal guardianship marriage). Note: In Shia fiqh, 'saghir' here often refers to a boy who has not reached the age of financial independence or is a minor under paternal guardianship (nikah al-ab). The text clarifies the financial liability based on 'mal' (wealth). Let's stick to the literal 'young/minor' but the key term is the concept of liability. Better key term: الْمَهْرُ (Dower). Let's refine key terms to be more precise from the text."
